\\frac{t_{\\rm max}}{\\tau_{\\rm sd}}\\approx\\frac{2\\Omega_{\\rm np}^{\\rm max}}{ \\Omega}\\approx 2.4\\times 10^{-3}\\,\\frac{PB_{12}^{1/2}}{\\varpi_{6}}  which features the interior B field. When applied to a typical radio pulsar (P=0.1\\,{\\rm s}, B=10^{12}\\,{\\rm G}) the result (29) leads to t_{\\rm max}\\ll\\tau_{\\rm sd}. Thus, core vortex pinning in a typical pulsar is an ephemeral phenomenon, plausibly associated with large glitches (Glampedakis & Andersson, 2009).

